				Usercat.DOC


This document describes  the format of  the user-defined catalog, which must
be in a file called  "usercat.dat" located in the directory specified by the
environment variable "USER_PATH".

The first  record in the catalog is the name of the catalog itself; the only
use made  of this information  is in generating headings  for the PostScript
printable  representation of the sky display  via the  "Chart" button.  This
name may be up to 80 characters in length.

The second record in the catalog is the epoch of the object positions.  This
line will  usually be  the current  year or  one  of "2000.0",  "1950.0", or
"1975.0".

The remainder of the catalog consists of one record per user-defined object;
the catalog can  define up to  200 objects.  Each record is composed of four
fields, delimited by backslashes.

The first field is some sort of user-specified object number; this number is
the ultimate identification  of this object inside xsky, and  therefore must
be  unique.  If this  field is  missing  (the  user  record  begins  with  a
backslash),  xsky  will generate  sequential object numbers beginning at  1.
The value of  this field must  be in the range  0 - 65535.  A user-requested
"Find" action can specify  the value in  this field, and this number will be
used to generate an object label if no label is given in the third field.

The  second field  is the position of the object,  in RA and declination, at
the epoch given by the second record of the catalog.  The general format is

		nnh nnm nns.nnn, snnd nn' nn".nn

with the value before the comma being RA in  hours,  minutes, and seconds of
time (specifiable  to thousandths), and  the  value after  the  comma  being
declination in signed  degrees,  minutes, and seconds of arc (specifiable to
hundredths).  Leading zeroes  in any field  may be  suppressed, and unneeded
decimals (including the decimal point itself) may be omitted.  This field is
required.

The third field is the label of the object, which xsky will use whenever the
user  executes  the pointer action for object label generation.  The  labels
will  also be searched  when the  user  attempts to "Find"  an object.  This
field may be  up to 20 characters in length; if it is unspecified,  the user
number will be  used as the object label.  Do not leave this field empty; if
no label is supplied, put at least one blank in this field.

The  fourth and  final field  is  the object  description.   This field  may
contain  arbitrary text up to 400 characters  in length.  This  text will be
formatted and displayed when the user executes the pointer action for object
catalog description.

